# Day 2 - Advanced Git & GitHub Management

## 1. Git Reset

Git Reset moves the current branch to another commit.

### Soft Reset

Keeps changes staged and in the working directory.

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

### Hard Reset

Deletes changes completely.

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

---

## 2. Git Revert

Git Revert creates a **new commit** that reverses the changes of an earlier commit.

```bash
git revert <commit-id>
```

It is safer for shared/public branches.

---

## 3. Reset vs Revert

| Reset                  | Revert                    |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Changes commit history | Keeps history             |
| Can remove commits     | Creates a new undo commit |
| Useful for local work  | Safe for shared branches  |

---

## 4. Squash Commit

Squashing combines multiple commits into one clean commit.

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~3
```

Change `pick` to `squash` for the commits you want to combine.

---

## 5. Cherry Pick

Cherry-pick applies a specific commit from another branch.

```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-id>
```

Useful when you need only one particular change.

---

## 6. Git Rebase

Rebase moves or reapplies commits on top of another branch.

```bash
git switch new
git rebase main
```

It creates a cleaner, linear history.

---

## 7. Git Merge

Merge combines changes from one branch into another.

```bash
git switch main
git merge new
```

It preserves the history of both branches.

---

## 8. Rebase vs Merge

| Rebase                          | Merge                             |
| ------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Creates linear history          | Preserves branch history          |
| Rewrites commit history         | Does not rewrite existing commits |
| Good for cleaning local history | Good for shared branches          |

